<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nizamuddin Auliya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1238">1238</a>–<a href="/years/1325">1325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Nizamuddin Awliya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nizamuddin Auliya was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint of the Chishti order who lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> for most of his life under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. Teaching love, humility and service to the poor, he drew followers from every class, including the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a>. His shrine in the Nizamuddin district of Delhi remains one of the most visited Sufi shrines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nizamuddin-auliya.jpg" alt="Nizamuddin Auliya" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NIZAM_AL-DIN_AWLIYA_GRASPING_A_SWORD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nizamuddin Auliya was born in 1238 into a Sayyid family at Badayun. At five, after his father's death, he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> with his mother, Bibi Zulekha. When he was sixteen or seventeen he first heard the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baba-farid">Fariduddin Ganjshakar</a>, known as Baba Farid, and love and respect arose in his heart. At twenty he went to Ajodhan and became Baba Farid's disciple, but kept studying theology in Delhi and visited Ajodhan each year for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a>. On his third visit Baba Farid made him his successor, and soon after he learned that Baba Farid had died.</p><p>Nizamuddin lived at several places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> before settling at Ghiyaspur, a neighbourhood away from the noise of the city, where he built a khanqah. People from all walks of life were fed and taught there, rich and poor alike. He had more than 600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">khalifas</a>, disciples authorised to take disciples of their own, among them Nasiruddin Chiragh Dehlavi, his spiritual successor, the singer and poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusro</a>, and Hasan Sijzi, who compiled his discourses as Fawaid ul Fuad. He did not marry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Sultan Qutbu'd-Din Mubarak Shah, angered that Nizamuddin had made Khizr Khan his disciple, forbade his nobles to visit Ghiyaspur and ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a> to pray at the mosque he built. The Shaikh refused, and sent a servant to the monthly gathering at the palace in his place. The sultan was assassinated on 8 July 1320, before the first day of the next month.</p><p>Like his predecessors, Nizamuddin stressed love as a means of realising God, and for him love of God implied love of humanity. He emphasised renunciation and trust in God, the unity of mankind without distinctions of status, helping the needy and feeding the hungry. He strongly disapproved of mixing with sultans, princes and nobles. He held sema, devotional listening, permissible only without musical instruments and dancing, and he discouraged displays of miracles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 3 April 1325. His shrine, the Nizamuddin Dargah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, was built in its present form in 1562 and is visited by people of all faiths. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusro</a>, who died within a few months of his master, is buried at his feet. Nizamuddin is regarded as the founder of the Chishti Nizami order.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nizamuddin Auliya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Delhi became a refuge for scholars, artisans and Sufis fleeing the Mongols, and the Chishti Sufi order, led in Delhi by <b>Nizamuddin Auliya</b>, flourished under its rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Budaun, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.05,79.12&amp;year=1294" aria-label="Budaun on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Nizamuddin Auliya"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4184 5128h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4184 5128h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=28.05,79.12&amp;year=1294">What was here in 1294? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
